Article 26
In the case of trial proceedings related to cases of violence against children, the presence of the child does not necessarily have to be presented at the trial, except in circumstances that are very compelling while still separating the meeting between the child and the perpetrator in trial processes at different times.

Article 27
In the case where the perpetrator of a criminal act is a child, the following matters apply:
a. the legal process is carried out humanely by considering that the child still has hope for their future;
b. The investigator must be a person who has competence in special investigation for child handling cases;
c. The investigation is conducted in a closed room and only attended by the advocacy team and the child's parents/guardian;
d. in the investigation process, the child is not placed in general detention, but rather entrusted to their family with supervision and safeguarding;
e. the trial process is carried out specifically in a juvenile court room; and
f. the sentencing process for the child is placed in a special juvenile correctional institution, with a transition period that can be accompanied by their parents.

CHAPTER XII
INVESTIGATION
Article 23 (1) In addition to general investigation officials, investigation of criminal acts of violence against women and children in this Regional Regulation may also be carried out by Civil Servant Investigators whose appointment is in accordance with the legislation. (2) Civil Servant Investigators as referred to in paragraph (1), are authorized to:
a. receive reports or complaints from a person regarding the existence of criminal acts of violence against women and children;
b. take initial action at that time at the scene and conduct examination; c. interrogate a suspect and examine the suspect's identity;
d. seize objects and/or documents; e. take fingerprints and photograph a person; f. summon persons to be heard and examined as suspects or witnesses;
g. bring in expert witnesses required in connection with the examination of the case;
h. terminate the investigation after receiving instruction from the General Investigator that there is insufficient evidence, or the incident does not constitute a criminal act and subsequently through the General Investigator notify this matter to the public prosecutor, the suspect or their family; and/or
i. take other actions necessary for the smooth investigation of criminal acts of violence against women and children, which according to law can be accounted for.
(3) Investigators as referred to in paragraph (2), shall notify the commencement of the investigation and submit the results of their investigation to the public prosecutor through the Investigating Official of the Police of the Republic of Indonesia, in accordance with the provisions stipulated in the Criminal Procedure Law.

Article 3
Paragraph (1) what is meant by "physical violence" is any act that results in pain, injury, wounds or disability to a person's body, miscarriage, fainting and or causes death. What is meant by "psychological violence" is an act that results in fear, loss of self-confidence, loss of ability to act, feelings of helplessness and or severe psychological suffering to a person. What is meant by "sexual violence" is any act in the form of sexual harassment, forced sexual relations, forced sexual relations in an unnatural or unwanted manner, forced sexual relations with others for commercial purposes and or certain purposes. What is meant by "exploitation" includes: acts that exploit a child economically or sexually with the intention of benefiting oneself or others;
acts with or without the consent of the victim that
b.
include but are not limited to prostitution, forced labor or services, slavery or similar practices, oppression, extortion, exploitation of physical, sexual, reproductive organs, or unlawfully removing or transplanting organs and/or body tissues or exploiting the labor or ability of a person by another party to obtain benefits both material and immaterial.
c. all forms of exploitation of sexual organs or other body organs of the victim to obtain benefits, including but not limited to all activities of prostitution or sexual abuse.
What is meant by "trafficking in persons" is the act of recruitment, transportation, harboring, sending, transfer, or receipt of a person by threat of violence, use of violence, abduction, confinement, forgery, fraud, abuse of power or position of vulnerability, debt bondage or giving payment or benefits, so as to obtain consent from the person holding control over that other person, whether conducted within a country or between countries, for the purpose of exploitation or resulting in a person being exploited.
What is meant by "Household Neglect" is: an act that results in the non-fulfillment of a child's needs properly, whether physical, mental, spiritual or social;
b. an act of deliberately neglecting to maintain, care for, or look after a child as appropriate;
c. an act that neglects a person within the scope of their household, whereas according to the law applicable to them or because of an agreement or contract they are obliged to provide life, care, or maintenance to that person;
d. an act that results in economic dependence by means of limiting and/or prohibiting proper work inside or outside the home so that the victim is under the control of that person. What is meant is deviant behavior of children such as the use of glue/aibon that can cause children to feel addicted or intoxicated because they are invited by their friends, and deviation towards free sex behavior or the use of prohibited items.
